## TIẾNG ANH · 16:30–17:00 — Thứ Năm: Câu hỏi phỏng vấn

### 16:30–16:40 · Shadowing

### 16:40–16:55 · Trả lời có ghi âm

**Câu hỏi tuần này:** *"How do push notifications work end to end?"*

> *"Your server doesn't talk to the device directly. It sends to Expo's push service, which fans out to APNs for iOS and FCM for Android, and those deliver to the device. The device registers and gets a token, which you store server-side and associate with the user — keyed on the token, not the user, because one person can have several devices.*
> *A few things matter in practice. Delivery is best effort — the phone might be off, or the user might have notifications disabled — so you never use push as a data sync mechanism. It's a nudge, not a transport.*
> *You have to handle the error responses. DeviceNotRegistered means the user uninstalled or turned notifications off, and if you don't delete those tokens your table slowly fills with devices that don't exist.*
> *Permission timing is the product side of it. On iOS the system dialog appears exactly once, so asking on launch — before the user knows what the app does — is the fastest way to a permanent no. I show my own explainer when they enable reminders, and only trigger the OS prompt if they agree.*
> *And I measure opt-out rate more closely than open rate. A high open rate with a climbing opt-out rate means you're burning trust, and there's no way to ask for it back."*

### 16:55–17:00 · 5 từ mới

| Từ / cụm | Nghĩa | Câu mẫu |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `nudge` (n) | cú huých nhẹ | *It's a nudge, not a transport.* |
| `opt out` | chọn không nhận nữa | *Opt-out rate matters more.* |
| `burn trust` | đốt lòng tin | *You're burning trust.* |
| `keyed on` | lấy làm khóa | *Keyed on the token, not the user.* |
| `a permanent no` | một lời từ chối vĩnh viễn | *The fastest way to a permanent no.* |

